--- body ---
## Context
The author asked to search his library scoped by **year**, and named
**author**, **title** and **doctype** alongside it. Two measurements already
bear on this and they point opposite ways, so the first job of this ticket is
to keep them apart.

**X4 does not block this.** X4 measured one mechanism — an arbitrary rowid set
shipped through `json_each` — and found it dominated by not scoping at all. That
is the mechanism a *collection* or a *tag* needs, because their membership is a
set the index does not store. It was reported to the author as though it closed
the door on scoping generally; it does not, and that misreading is what this
ticket exists to correct in code.

**A stored attribute is affordable, measured.** `bench/results/0025-year-scope/`
runs each scope both ways over the same items and the same queries on the real
477 512-passage index: an indexed year predicate answers where the `json_each`
blob is 43x slower, and against the same run's unconstrained baseline the filter
is free to within noise — a five-year and a ten-year scope both come out
*cheaper* than no filter, because the predicate removes work.

**The blocker is not latency. The index stores no date.** `items` is
`(item_key, title)`; `passages` carries `(pid, id, item_key, title, text,
source, vector)`. There is no date, no creator, no item type. So three of the
four facets asked for are not slow today — they are impossible. Adding a year
column and its indexes over the whole index cost 529 ms in the probe, and
Zotero's local API already returns `meta.parsedDate` and `data.itemType`, so the
build has the values in hand.

**Upstream takes no filters at all.** `zotero_semantic_search`'s input schema is
`q`, `limit`, `mode`, `auto_build` — nothing else — and its own description
redirects field filtering to `zotero_search_items`, which filters but does not
rank semantically and which measured at seconds per query, unranked
(ticket 0025). So the capability needs an API surface as well as a column.

## Where each facet stands against the sheet
This matters because two of the four are R5 clauses and two are not, and the
difference is the author's to rule on rather than ours to assume.

| facet | in R5's letter? | stored today? |
|---|---|---|
| year / date | **yes** — R5 names date | no |
| item type (doctype) | **yes** — R5 names item type | no |
| author / creator | **no** | no |
| title | **no** | yes, on both `items` and `passages` |

R5 enumerates "collection, tag, item type, or date". Author and title are
outside its letter. Title is already stored and already searchable as text, so
"filter by title" may mean something different from the other three — a field
restriction rather than a scope — and that should be settled before it is built.
Extending R5 to name creator, or filing a sibling requirement, is a ruling for
`DECISIONS.md` and is NOT decided here.

R5's constraint on *how* is already satisfied by the measured shape: the filter
is applied while ranking, so truncation happens after it, which is what R5's
"before any answer is truncated" demands. R5 forbids post-filtering a top-k that
claims completeness; it does not require constraining MATCH, and says so.

## Relevant files
- `bench/year_scope.mjs` — the measurement, both mechanisms, with the control.
- `bench/harvest_years.py` — pulls `meta.parsedDate` from the local API.
- `bench/results/0025-year-scope/year-vs-json-each.json` — the artifact.
- `DECISIONS.md` — carries both awaiting-ratification entries: X4's ladder
  consequence, and the stored-attribute entry this ticket implements.
- `SPEC.md` §3 R5 — the promise; R18 — what an empty scope must say.
- Upstream `src/features/search/sqlite-index.ts` (schema), `build.ts` (where item
  metadata is written), `src/tools/semantic-search.ts` (the input schema).

## Actions
1. Settle the scope question first, since two facets are unratified: put the
   author/title reading to the author and record the ruling. Do not build a
   creator filter against a requirement that does not name one.
2. Add the columns the facets need to the index schema — at minimum `year` and
   `item_type` on `items`, `creator` if ruling 1 admits it — populated at build
   time from data the crawl already fetches. Note this is a schema change, and
   upstream has no migration path (issue #34): every user re-embeds unless that
   is addressed first, which is an argument for landing this *with* a migration
   rather than before one.
3. Extend the query path to take an optional filter and apply it while ranking,
   never after truncation.
4. Surface the filter on `zotero_semantic_search`'s input schema.
5. Answer R18 honestly for a scoped query: when the ranked stream is exhausted
   before k is filled, say "this scope is not indexed yet" or "nothing matches"
   *for the scope asked about*. This is the rung that catches what the filter
   cannot, and X4's verdict made it load-bearing.

## Test
Red first, on a fixture index carrying two items of different years: a query
scoped to the year of the non-matching item must return zero hits AND a
`scope{}` block saying which — not an empty list. That test fails today for the
strongest possible reason, which is that no filter argument exists to write.

## Verification
- [ ] A year-scoped query returns only items of that year, on the real library
- [ ] The filter is applied before truncation, shown by a case where an
      unscoped top-k contains none of the in-scope hits
- [ ] p95 for a scoped query stays within the unscoped baseline's band on the
      real index (the probe measured no marginal cost; this re-checks it in the
      server rather than in SQL)
- [ ] An empty scoped result carries R18's distinction
- [ ] `make check` green

## Invariants
- X4's verdict stands: no `json_each` rowid-set step ships. This ticket adds a
  different mechanism, it does not retry that one.
- R5's "before any answer is truncated" is not weakened to a post-filter.
- Coverage honesty (R4, R17) survives: a scoped answer must not read as complete
  when the scope is only partly indexed.
- Nothing here is filed upstream without the author's authorization.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] The author/title reading is ruled on in `DECISIONS.md`
- [ ] Year and item-type scoping work end to end against the real library
- [ ] R18's scope block answers an exhausted scope
- [ ] The stored-attribute ledger entry is ratified and SPEC.md §5.2.2 edited to match
